// The engine's features, as the demo page at https://jabbott.dev/Nadir/
// lists them below the demo (ADR-0029).
//
// Each feature names the roadmap tasks it needs (`docs/roadmap.md`). The
// page marks it shipped when all of them are ticked, in progress when some
// are, and planned when none are. `cargo xtask features`, part of
// `cargo xtask ci`, fails when a ticked task belongs to no feature, or when a
// feature's `ticked` is not what the roadmap ticks: the task that ships
// something adds it to a feature here, or describes a new one, and brings
// its feature's summary up to date.
//
// - `name`: a few words.
// - `area`: Core, Platform, Graphics, Simulation, Content, Audio, Tools,
//   Quality or Release.
// - `summary`: what it does, for a reader who has not seen the code, in a
//   sentence or three. What the ticked tasks built is in the present tense,
//   and what is still to come in the future tense, so a planned feature's
//   summary says what it will do. A task that is ticked rewrites its
//   feature's summary to match. Speak of future work only from the
//   feature's own `tasks`, and give no count that another feature's tasks
//   will change: the check cannot tell a summary that another feature's
//   task was ticked.
// - `tasks`: the roadmap task IDs.
// - `ticked`: those of `tasks` that were ticked when `summary` was written;
//   left out when none were. The check fails when it is not what the
//   roadmap ticks, so ticking a task means rewriting its feature's summary.
{
  "features": [
    {
      "name": "No third-party crates",
      "area": "Quality",
      "summary": "The game is built from this workspace's crates alone: its OS bindings, OpenGL backend, math, ECS, codecs and tools are written here, and beyond Rust's standard library it calls only the system's own libraries, such as Xlib, EGL and OpenGL. The build fails if the lock file gains a package from outside the workspace, and each crate may use only the crates the architecture's dependency matrix allows.",
      "tasks": ["M0.1", "M0.6"],
      "ticked": ["M0.1", "M0.6"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Guardrails on every push",
      "area": "Quality",
      "summary": "Each push is formatted, linted with warnings as errors, tested, and checked for dependencies, layering and unsafe code on Windows, macOS and Linux. Unsafe code is allowed in three crates only, and the audit fails any unsafe block without a SAFETY comment.",
      "tasks": ["M0.6", "M0.8"],
      "ticked": ["M0.6", "M0.8"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Fuzzing and benchmarks",
      "area": "Quality",
      "summary": "A mutation fuzzer of our own runs each parser of the codec crate in CI, with a hang watchdog, and saves an input that panics or hangs one. A benchmark harness reports medians and 95th percentiles against a recorded baseline.",
      "tasks": ["M0.7"],
      "ticked": ["M0.7"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Math, color and geometry",
      "area": "Core",
      "summary": "Vectors, 3 × 3 matrices, 2D affine transforms, rectangles, and colors with sRGB conversions, checked by property tests such as inverse round trips and symmetric intersections.",
      "tasks": ["M0.2"],
      "ticked": ["M0.2"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Handles, hashing and random numbers",
      "area": "Core",
      "summary": "Generational arenas and typed handles that catch a stale reference, FNV-1a, a fast hasher for maps, CRC-32 and the PCG32 generator, each checked against reference values.",
      "tasks": ["M0.3"],
      "ticked": ["M0.3"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Logging, time and test tools",
      "area": "Core",
      "summary": "Log macros with rotating files and an in-memory ring, calendar time without the OS, a command-line parser, profile scopes, and a property-test helper that prints the seed of a failure so that it can be run again.",
      "tasks": ["M0.4"],
      "ticked": ["M0.4"]
    },
    {
      "name": "JSON and JSONC",
      "area": "Content",
      "summary": "A parser and writer for JSON, with comments and trailing commas allowed in JSONC. Bad input gets an error with its line and column, never a panic, and limits on depth, sizes and counts keep a hostile file from exhausting memory.",
      "tasks": ["M0.5"],
      "ticked": ["M0.5"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Native windows on every desktop",
      "area": "Platform",
      "summary": "Windows, keys, mouse, focus, resizing, DPI scaling and borderless fullscreen through each system's own API, declared by hand. Linux, through X11 loaded at run time, is done. The Win32 and Cocoa versions build and pass CI, and will be done once they are checked by hand on Windows and macOS.",
      "tasks": ["M1.1", "M1.2", "M1.5", "M1.6"],
      "ticked": ["M1.1", "M1.2"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Fixed-tick main loop",
      "area": "Simulation",
      "summary": "The simulation steps exactly 60 times a second whatever the frame rate, and each frame draws between the last two ticks, so motion is smooth at 60, 144 or uncapped frames a second. A tap between two ticks still counts, and a long stall is clamped instead of spiraling into catch-up ticks.",
      "tasks": ["M1.3", "M3.4"],
      "ticked": ["M1.3", "M3.4"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Log files and fatal errors",
      "area": "Platform",
      "summary": "On Linux the game finds its folders where the system expects them, writes each run's log there and keeps the last three, and logs and reports an error that ends the game before it exits. Windows and macOS will follow, with their windows.",
      "tasks": ["M1.4", "M1.5", "M1.6"],
      "ticked": ["M1.4"]
    },
    {
      "name": "OpenGL 3.3 on every desktop",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "An OpenGL 3.3 core context with vsync through EGL on Linux, and an offscreen context that the tests and these recordings draw with. WGL on Windows and NSOpenGL on macOS will follow.",
      "tasks": ["M2.1", "M2.2", "M2.6", "M2.7"],
      "ticked": ["M2.1", "M2.2"]
    },
    {
      "name": "A small graphics interface",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "A thin layer over OpenGL 3.3 core: textures, buffers, render targets, pipelines, instanced draws, scissor and readback, with a state cache. Using a destroyed resource is an error rather than a wrong frame, and a shader that fails to compile reports the driver's log.",
      "tasks": ["M2.3"],
      "ticked": ["M2.3"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Golden-image tests",
      "area": "Quality",
      "summary": "Scenes of the renderer are drawn offscreen on Mesa's software renderer in CI and compared with committed reference images within a small tolerance. A scene that differs uploads what it drew and an image of where.",
      "tasks": ["M2.4", "M2.5"],
      "ticked": ["M2.4", "M2.5"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Sprites, y-sorting and a pixel-perfect camera",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "Sprites are sorted by layer and by where their feet are, so what stands lower on screen is drawn in front, then packed into instanced batches. A 2D camera draws at a fixed virtual resolution and scales it up by whole numbers, letterboxed, so every pixel stays square.",
      "tasks": ["M3.3"],
      "ticked": ["M3.3"]
    },
    {
      "name": "10,000 sprites with no allocation",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "The stress scene draws 10,203 sprites in 1 batch, all from one page of the cooked atlas. A test counts every allocation the engine makes through Rust's allocator in the real loop and finds none in 100 steady frames, and sorting and batching such a frame takes about 0.74 ms on an i7-11800H.",
      "tasks": ["M3.5"],
      "ticked": ["M3.5"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Input actions",
      "area": "Simulation",
      "summary": "Keys and mouse buttons map to named actions, buttons or 2D axes such as WASD, latched once a tick so that nothing pressed between ticks is lost. A text mode mutes the game's bindings while a text field has the keyboard, and the actions can be recorded for replays.",
      "tasks": ["M3.1"],
      "ticked": ["M3.1"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Asset loading",
      "area": "Content",
      "summary": "Typed handles to assets loaded from files: the same path gives the same handle, and a development build shows a placeholder for anything missing. Worker threads read and decode the files while the game runs, and each frame makes the decoded ones within a budget of 2 ms: asked for a thousand at once, an optimized build spends under 4 ms of any frame on them. Assets load in scopes, global, level or transient, and unloading a scope frees what no other scope holds, GPU textures included.",
      "tasks": ["M3.2", "M5.3"],
      "ticked": ["M3.2", "M5.3"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Entity component system",
      "area": "Simulation",
      "summary": "Generational entities with sparse-set storage, resources, events, deferred commands and a schedule of stages with run conditions; a borrow conflict names the system that caused it. The engine's own systems move, collide, animate and draw entities.",
      "tasks": ["M4.1", "M4.4"],
      "ticked": ["M4.1", "M4.4"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Reflection",
      "area": "Simulation",
      "summary": "Macros describe structs and enums as dynamic values and back, with a registry of types and warnings for unknown fields. Asset handles are written as their files' paths and loaded again when read, and the engine's sprites, colliders and animators are reflected too: a sprite can name a frame of a cooked atlas, a collider its layers by name, an animator its clip. The determinism tests hash worlds through it.",
      "tasks": ["M4.2"],
      "ticked": ["M4.2"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Collision and sliding movement",
      "area": "Simulation",
      "summary": "Boxes and circles, tile grids and a spatial hash, with move-and-slide in up to 16 substeps so that movers follow their path and slide along walls without snagging, plus sweeps, triggers and queries. 5,000 colliding entities tick in about 2.4 ms on an i7-11800H.",
      "tasks": ["M4.3", "M4.8"],
      "ticked": ["M4.3", "M4.8"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Tilemaps",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "Tile layers drawn a chunk at a time from a cache, culled to the view, with animated tiles. Tall tiles become y-sorted sprites, and the same map gives the collision grid: the field in the demo is one.",
      "tasks": ["M4.5"],
      "ticked": ["M4.5"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Sprite animation",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "Clips of frames that loop, or play once and send an event at the end, played by an Animator component and mirrored by the way the entity faces.",
      "tasks": ["M4.6"],
      "ticked": ["M4.6"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Deterministic replays",
      "area": "Quality",
      "summary": "The input is recorded tick by tick and played back with no window. A test replays a stored recording and compares a hash of the reflected world with the stored sequence, so a change that makes the simulation nondeterministic fails it. The hash leaves out what animation rewrites each frame, a sprite's frame and the way it faces.",
      "tasks": ["M4.7"],
      "ticked": ["M4.7"]
    },
    {
      "name": "QOI and PNG images",
      "area": "Content",
      "summary": "QOI encoding and decoding for the game's textures, and a PNG decoder, with an inflate and Adler-32 of its own, for the asset cooker. Each has hand-made test vectors and a fuzz target.",
      "tasks": ["M2.4", "M5.1"],
      "ticked": ["M2.4", "M5.1"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Compression, binary JSON, fonts and WAV",
      "area": "Content",
      "summary": "KLZ, an LZ4-style block compression, and KBJ, a binary JSON with one encoding per value, both of which round-trip; and readers for BMFont's text format, as AngelCode, Hiero and other tools write it, and for WAV. All four reject malformed input and run under the fuzzer.",
      "tasks": ["M5.2"],
      "ticked": ["M5.2"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Asset cooking",
      "area": "Tools",
      "summary": "An offline pipeline packs each folder's PNG images, Aseprite sprite sheet exports and bitmap fonts into an atlas: it trims each frame and glyph to its pixels, packs them into square pages with padding and extruded edges, keeps tile sets as pages of their own, and writes QOI pages with a file of where each frame went and the animation clips of the exports' tags, which it checks with the game's own reader, and a file of each font's glyphs. Data files become KBJ. The same files cook to the same bytes, and an incremental cache cooks again only what changed. The game loads an atlas before its first frame and finds its sprites and clips by name, each trimmed frame placed, and mirrored, where its whole cell would be: the demo's walking person comes from an export in Aseprite's format, written by hand, drawn pixel for pixel as before.",
      "tasks": ["M5.4", "M5.9", "M5.5"],
      "ticked": ["M5.4", "M5.9", "M5.5"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Levels and prefabs",
      "area": "Content",
      "summary": "Prefabs inherit from one another: a prefab's components apply over its base's, and a level's overrides over both, onto one value, and a cycle, a chain of more than 16, an unknown component or a value that does not fit is refused, naming the file and the field, with no entity left behind. A level's entities spawn through their prefabs. The engine reads LDtk 1.5.3's projects and level files, and its own level and prefab files, strictly and under the fuzzer: a project saved by a version of LDtk other than 1.5.x is refused with the version to save it with, and every refusal names where in the file it is. The cook turns each level of an LDtk project into a level file, by conventions for what LDtk has no notion of: animated and tall tiles from a tile set's custom data, the grid from the Collision layer, and each entity the prefab of its name, its fields overriding the prefab's. What a level file cannot hold, such as a translucent layer or two tiles in one cell, is refused, naming the file and the member. A level loads whole, in place of the last: the engine draws its tile layers over its background, collides with its grid, spawns its tall tiles and its entities, and removes what it made when the next comes. The demo's field is an LDtk project, cooked to the map it was built from, cell for cell, with its trees and rocks as prefabs, and the game now draws it from its level file, frame for frame as it drew the map it built by hand.",
      "tasks": ["M5.10", "M5.11", "M5.12", "M5.6"],
      "ticked": ["M5.10", "M5.11", "M5.12", "M5.6"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Pack files",
      "area": "Content",
      "summary": "The cook writes every file it cooks into one pack, each file of the same bytes stored once, aligned, and compressed where that makes it smaller, and reads it back with the game's reader before writing it. The engine reads packs strictly and under the fuzzer, through the same virtual file system as loose files: a header and a table of contents checked against the limits, and the table against its CRC-32, before anything is allocated, each file found by a binary search of its path's hash, and each file's bytes checked against their CRC-32 as it is read, so that a damaged one is refused, naming its path. Release builds read the pack when there is one, and the loose files otherwise, and the release files and the download ship the pack alone; a pack that does not open fails every load with its error, and development builds read the loose files.",
      "tasks": ["M5.13", "M5.14", "M5.7"],
      "ticked": ["M5.13", "M5.14", "M5.7"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Hot reload",
      "area": "Tools",
      "summary": "An edit saved while the sandbox runs in a development build, beside the cook's watch, is drawn in its window within half a second: a sprite's pixels, an Aseprite export's clips, a prefab, the LDtk level, or a shader. The cook, built once, cooks the edit again in about a fifth of a second and rewrites only the files whose bytes changed; the game's watchers look at the loaded files and the shaders every 250 ms. Each reload lands behind the handles the game holds: a texture draws its new texels, an atlas's clips are replaced by name, a prefab is replaced in its library, and the level is made again around its tile layers, clips and assets, its props spawned anew. A file that fails to load, or a shader that does not compile or fit its program, is logged, and what it would replace stays. With nothing changed, a frame allocates nothing, and release builds hold none of it. On Windows and macOS it comes with drawing there.",
      "tasks": ["M5.15", "M5.16", "M5.17", "M5.8"],
      "ticked": ["M5.15", "M5.16", "M5.17", "M5.8"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Audio mixer",
      "area": "Audio",
      "summary": "Sound is compressed as QOA, with an encoder and decoder of our own, to 3.2 bits a sample: a tone keeps 71 dB of signal over its noise, and white noise 16, and a damaged or cut file is refused, not played. The cook turns each WAV below the assets' audio folder into 16-bit PCM in a sound file of our own, or into QOA, as its folder or a sidecar says, with music in QOA and looped. It resamples each to 48 kHz with a windowed sinc of our own, which keeps a tone's error 92 dB below it up to 0.82 of the Nyquist frequency, resamples a loop's seam as any other point, and is built to give the same bits on every system, and it warns of a sound that clips or that ends loud enough to click. The asset server loads them as sounds and music on its workers, a sound converted to 48 kHz by the same sinc unless it may be a loop, a failed one silent where placeholders are on, and the mixer plays each by its handle; a level that loads its music again goes on playing it. A mixer of our own mixes up to 48 voices on four buses, resampled as their rate or pitch asks, with constant-power panning, attenuation by distance from the listener, fades of at least 2 ms at every start and stop, voices stolen by priority, a soft limiter, and music streamed from QOA a frame at a time, crossfading from one track to the next. It allocates nothing as it mixes: 32 voices and a track take 88 µs of a 10.7 ms callback, and it is tested offline, frame by frame. The engine plays the game's sound through it with nothing to set up: sounds that play at their entities, panned and faded as they move about the listener, which is the camera unless an entity is, and music that crossfades as the listener goes from one area to another, each bus at the volume the game's config gives it, while tests, headless runs and captures stay silent. The sandbox has footsteps that follow the player, a lapping pond, and a tune for each half of its field, all sounds of our own. A soak of our own plays 32 random voices and two tracks of music through the audio device for ten minutes, or mixes them all and gives the device silence, and tells of each underrun and each callback later than the device's buffer, the 99th percentile of the time a mix takes, the process's memory, and whether the mixer handed back every sound it played: a minute of it here takes 198 µs at the 99th percentile of a 7.5 ms period, with no underrun and no growth in memory.",
      "tasks": ["M6.1", "M6.2", "M6.8", "M6.9", "M6.6", "M6.7"],
      "ticked": ["M6.1", "M6.2", "M6.8", "M6.9", "M6.6", "M6.7"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Audio output on every desktop",
      "area": "Platform",
      "summary": "On Linux, sound goes out through ALSA, which the engine loads while it runs, on an audio thread of its own: about 30 ms from the mix to the device, with each underrun recovered and counted. A device that is unplugged, busy, slow to open or not there yet is logged once and tried again every second, while what plays keeps its time, and the game never waits on the device more than 2 s, to start or to quit. Windows will play through WASAPI and macOS through AudioUnit, behind the same API.",
      "tasks": ["M6.3", "M6.4", "M6.5"],
      "ticked": ["M6.3"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Bitmap text",
      "area": "Graphics",
      "summary": "Text is drawn from bitmap fonts made with BMFont or Hiero: wrapped at a width, aligned left, in the middle or right, kerned, colored in runs with a small markup, and with a fallback glyph for a character a font lacks. Glyphs are a mask in the text's color, or keep their own colors for outlined fonts, and land on whole pixels. Text goes in the world, or on the window in a UI with sprites, drawn after the world at the window's resolution in the order it is pushed: in the game's own pixels or at the settings' UI scale, each window cut to its clip, and the mouse mapped to it. The cook's font files are read by a reader of our own, which checks each one strictly, and the cook reads back every font file it writes; a fuzzer throws about a million damaged files a second at it.",
      "tasks": ["M7.7", "M7.1", "M7.8"],
      "ticked": ["M7.7", "M7.1", "M7.8"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Developer tools",
      "area": "Tools",
      "summary": "Development builds show a debug UI of our own over the game: windows the game adds, each shown by a key of its own, that drag, collapse, resize and scroll, with buttons, checkboxes, sliders, text fields, lists, trees, line graphs and color editors, drawn in the engine's own pixel font, and keeping their places across runs. A click is judged where it was pressed and where it was released at any frame rate, typing reaches it with key repeats and composed characters, and while it has the mouse or a text field the game's actions do not see them. The engine's own windows are a stats overlay of the frame rate, the frame's processor time, its ticks, draw calls, sprites, entities, allocations, voices and underruns, and a profiler of the last 300 frames: a graph of their processor time, the scopes of the frame clicked as a tree, from the engine's steps down to each system, and a Chrome trace of them all for Perfetto UI. The debug UI will carry the GPU's timings, an entity inspector, a console, debug drawing and time controls, all left out of release builds.",
      "tasks": ["M7.2", "M7.9", "M7.10", "M7.3", "M7.11", "M7.4", "M7.5", "M7.6"],
      "ticked": ["M7.2", "M7.9", "M7.10", "M7.3"]
    },
